winebus: an absence is the neutral request, not a zeroed struct.

mgvf-0009 stored "nobody asked anything of this pad" as a zeroed
struct dualsense_vibration, and the write paths tested for that. It made
{mode CLIENT, gain 0} -- a gain of zero written on purpose, which is the
request for SILENCE -- byte-identical to an absence. dualsense_usb.h promised
the opposite in so many words: "0 is not absence: it silences the motors, which
is how somebody who does not want the pad to buzz at all turns it off for every
game at once". That promise was not kept. Silence by gain alone reached no
motor, and mgvf-0014's guard is what read it as nothing.

IT WAS UNREACHABLE, AND THEN IT WAS NOT. This was found on 2026-09-10 and left
alone on purpose, because the only thing that writes these values -- this
project's launcher -- also wrote VibrationMode 1 beside its "Custom strength",
and {1, 0} is a request the guard lets through. The same day, the launcher
stopped doing that: a strength and a path turned out to be two questions, and
tying them meant nobody could ask for the game's own path at a chosen strength,
which is the combination mgvf-0020 made worth having. The moment "Custom
strength" wrote mode 0, silence became reachable and broken in the same change.

So the sentinel goes. An absence is {CLIENT, 100} -- keep the client's own mode,
leave every motor byte alone -- which is a REQUEST that asks for nothing rather
than a value that means nothing. Both entry points already refuse exactly that
pair on their own, so no new test is needed anywhere; dualsense_vibration_wanted
says what the write paths used to spell out, and says it once.

The one thing that had to be got right: a device extension must START from the
neutral pair. bus_create_hid_device assigns *vib only for a pad that has
options, and everything else was left as the allocator found it -- zeros. With
zeros now meaning silence, every device in the prefix would have been asking
for it. It is initialised explicitly instead, for every device, and the host
test asserts the neutral pair is the one a fresh device starts from.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
@@ -174,14 +174,13 @@
  *         somebody who does not want the pad to buzz at all turns it off for
  *         every game at once. Clamped to DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_GAIN_MAX.
  *
- *         SILENCE BY GAIN ALONE DOES NOT REACH THE PAD, and the paragraph
- *         above is what this warns about. A gain of 0 with no mode is {0, 0},
- *         which main.c's dualsense_vibration_options stores for "nothing asked
- *         for" as well, so mgvf-0014's guard cannot tell them apart and the
- *         motors are left alone. Asking for silence therefore means writing
- *         VibrationMode too -- which is what the launcher does. The functions
- *         below honour a bare gain of 0 correctly; it is the driver that never
- *         hands them one. */
+ *         SILENCE BY GAIN ALONE REACHES THE PAD since mgvf-0022, and the
+ *         paragraph above was a promise this file could not keep before it. A
+ *         gain of 0 with no mode is {0, 0}, which used to be exactly what
+ *         dualsense_vibration_options stored for "nothing asked for", so the
+ *         write path could not tell a request for silence from an absence and
+ *         left the motors alone. The absence is the neutral request now --
+ *         {CLIENT, 100} -- and a bare 0 is a request like any other. */
 #define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_MODE_CLIENT 0
 #define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_MODE_LEGACY 1
 #define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_GAIN_MAX    1000
@@ -191,6 +190,28 @@
     unsigned int mode;
     unsigned int gain;
 };
+/* MGVF mgvf-0022: what "nothing was asked for" looks like, and why it is not
+ * all zeros.
+ *
+ * It used to be. mgvf-0009 zeroed the struct for a pad with no options and the
+ * write paths tested for that, which made {mode CLIENT, gain 0} -- a gain of
+ * zero written on purpose, the request for SILENCE -- byte-identical to an
+ * absence. The comments in this file promised the opposite and were wrong for a
+ * day: silence by gain alone reached no motor. It was unreachable from the
+ * launcher only because its "Custom strength" also wrote mode 1, and it stopped
+ * being unreachable the moment a strength stopped dragging a path with it.
+ *
+ * So the absence is the neutral REQUEST instead: keep the client's own mode and
+ * leave every motor byte alone. Both entry points already refuse exactly that
+ * pair on their own, so it needs no separate test -- and {CLIENT, 0} is now a
+ * request like any other. Every device extension must start here rather than at
+ * zero, or a pad nobody asked anything of would read as a request for
+ * silence. */
+#define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_NONE { DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_MODE_CLIENT, 100 }
+static inline int dualsense_vibration_wanted(const struct dualsense_vibration *vib)
+{
+    return vib->mode != DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_MODE_CLIENT || vib->gain != 100;
+}
 
